      "math_academy_id": "q-335640",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?",
      "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, adding the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n0+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
